A Route describes how to construct an ordinary choice value. The caller can
supply the fields it already knows and reserve one field for later. Here's
the to-do row with an editor and a delete action.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    TodoMessage = {
        Rename | id I32, text String |,
        Delete | id I32 |,
    }

    row_label type Message |
        title Wire of String,
        on_rename Route of String -> Message,
        on_delete Route of Message,
    | -> String {
        return title
    }

    title ~= "Draft"
    row_id = 42
    label = row_label(
        title,
        TodoMessage::Rename(row_id),
        TodoMessage::Delete(row_id),
    )
    io.line(label)
]

This program prints `Draft`. The call also forms two routes. In the declaration,
`type Message` lets each caller choose a message type. Here both constructors
belong to `TodoMessage`, so Moth uses that type for `Message`.

Read the rename parameter from left to right. `String -> Message` leaves
one String for the final field of the chosen constructor. The caller
chooses `Rename` and supplies the row ID now. If the new text were
`"Buy milk"`, the corresponding message would be
`TodoMessage::Rename(42, "Buy milk")`.

Deleting a row needs only the ID. The second route captures it now, so
`Route of Message` has no input arrow. To prepare another row, the
caller passes that row's title and ID to the same function.

The `on_rename` and `on_delete` parameters give the constructors this
meaning. On its own, `message = TodoMessage::Rename(row_id)` would try
to build a complete message and fail because `text` is missing. The example
returns a label. Applying a route to a real event would require a UI consumer.
